      "Formula gains state (m) (mayStop), start (mayStop), transition (a) (bs) and final (m). state expands to and [after m, terminal or not terminal]; start is an atom, true where no message has been delivered yet (the model's initial node) and the stop bit is as given, so unfold leaves it as it is; transition a bs expands to follows a bs and final m to follows (state m true) [state m true]. state and start are atoms of the DAG (Dag.shape's default), evaluated by satTree, written by atomText as (state M), (state M stop), start and (start stop). transition and final are DAG operators over their state arguments: the s-expression writer prints a transition's arguments in table form (Dag.stateArg: a bare name, (Name stop), start, (start stop)), the DAG compiler and readOp read them as follows. The s-expression reader takes (state M), (state M stop), the bare start and (start stop), and (transition ...) and (final M) with arguments in table form, refusing any other word after a name; the JSON reader takes the same in JSON. unknownAtoms checks their messages as it checks on; nameText quotes a message named start. Faithful.labelFormula names a message's label with state and the start with start; Faithful.rules writes each typestate's rule as final when its only next typestate is itself and its name is a state that may stop, transition when its name and every next name are states or start, and follows otherwise, and names it typestate N (the label text stays in faithfulness verdicts, where it describes a state the formula does not show)."
